{"causes": ["The flourishing of the Magadhan kingdom under King Bimbisara provided a stable environment for the growth of new spiritual movements like Buddhism in the 6th century BCE.", "Gautama Buddha's establishment of the bhikkhuni order allowed women, including Kṣemā, to pursue spiritual liberation and leadership roles.", "King Bimbisara's persistent encouragement and strategic use of poets led Kṣemā to encounter the Buddha, initiating her path to enlightenment."], "effects": ["Kṣemā's attainment of arhatship provided a powerful example for women seeking spiritual liberation within the early Buddhist Sangha.", "Her renowned wisdom and teaching abilities contributed significantly to the spread and understanding of the Dharma among both monastics and lay followers.", "Kṣemā's stories and teachings, preserved in the Pāli Canon, became foundational texts, influencing the spiritual development of Buddhist practitioners for millennia."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "gautama-buddha", "sourceName": "Gautama Buddha", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "kem", "targetName": "Kṣemā", "context": "Gautama Buddha's teachings and skillful means directly influenced Kṣemā's attainment of arhatship in the 6th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "kem", "sourceName": "Kṣemā", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "early-buddhist-sangha", "targetName": "Early Buddhist Sangha", "context": "Kṣemā was a leading bhikkhuni and chief female disciple, playing a crucial role in the development and expansion of the early Buddhist monastic community."}, {"sourceSlug": "bimbisara", "sourceName": "King Bimbisara", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "kem", "targetName": "Kṣemā", "context": "King Bimbisara, Kṣemā's husband, influenced her spiritual journey by convincing her to visit the Buddha in the 6th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "kem", "sourceName": "Kṣemā", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-magadha", "targetName": "Ancient Magadha", "context": "Kṣemā's life as a queen and later as a bhikkhuni primarily occurred within the ancient Indian kingdom of Magadha during the 6th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "kem", "sourceName": "Kṣemā", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "uppalavanna", "targetName": "Uppalavanna", "context": "Kṣemā collaborated with Uppalavanna as the two principal female disciples of the Buddha, often teaching and inspiring other bhikkhunis."}], "places": [{"name": "Sagala, Ancient Madra", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Rajagriha, Ancient Magadha", "role": "Site of Enlightenment"}, {"name": "Veluvana Monastery, Ancient Magadha", "role": "Monastic Residence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Kṣemā", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "kṣemā"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2627201", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khema"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Kṣemā is remembered as a landmark person of the Classical era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse; Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Kṣemā's life and teachings were crucial in establishing the legitimacy and spiritual capacity of women within the early Buddhist Sangha, directly influencing the development of the bhikkhuni order. Her example provided a powerful model for female spiritual attainment and leadership that resonated across ancient India and continues to inspire practitioners.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-25T08:42:35Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45574441641", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Khema was a Buddhist bhikkhuni, or nun, who was one of the top female disciples of the Buddha. She is considered the first of the Buddha's two chief female disciples, along with Uppalavanna. Khema was born into the royal family of the ancient Kingdom of Madra, and was the wife of King Bimbisara of the ancient Indian kingdom of Magadha. Khema was convinced to visit the Buddha by her husband, who hired poets to sing about the beauty of the monastery he was staying at to her. She attained enlightenment as a laywoman while listening to one of the Buddha's sermons, considered a rare feat in Buddhist texts.", "newValue": "Kṣemā, also known as Khema, emerged in the 6th century BCE as one of the two principal female disciples of Gautama Buddha, a pivotal figure in the nascent Buddhist Sangha. Born into the royal family of the ancient Kingdom of Madra around 550 BCE in Sagala, she later became a queen consort to King Bimbisara of Magadha, one of the most powerful states in ancient India. Her spiritual journey and subsequent role as a leading bhikkhuni solidified the position of women within early Buddhism, demonstrating profound spiritual attainment was accessible regardless of gender or social standing.

Initially resistant to the Buddha's teachings due to her attachment to worldly beauty, Kṣemā was persuaded by King Bimbisara to visit the Veluvana monastery near Rajagriha. There, the Buddha employed a skillful teaching method, conjuring a celestial nymph whose beauty faded before her eyes, illustrating the impermanence of all physical forms. This profound experience led Kṣemā to directly perceive the truth of anicca (impermanence), dukkha (suffering), and anattā (non-self), culminating in her instantaneous attainment of arhatship and full enlightenment. She subsequently renounced her royal life and joined the order of bhikkhunis, dedicating herself to the propagation of the Dharma.

As one of the Buddha's two chief female disciples, alongside Uppalavanna, Kṣemā became renowned for her wisdom (paññā) and exceptional ability to teach and clarify complex doctrines. Her profound insights into the nature of existence and her eloquent expositions of the Dharma made her a revered figure, inspiring countless women and men to embrace the Buddhist path. Her stories, preserved in the Pāli Canon, particularly in the Therīgāthā and the Samyutta Nikaya, served as foundational texts for the education and spiritual development of the early Sangha."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-25T08:42:35Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45574441641", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "550 BCE, Sagala"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "550 BCE, Sagala"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing of the Magadhan kingdom under King Bimbisara provided a stable environment for the growth of new spiritual movements like Buddhism in the 6th century BCE.", "Gautama Buddha's establishment of the bhikkhuni order allowed women, including Kṣemā, to pursue spiritual liberation and leadership roles.", "King Bimbisara's persistent encouragement and strategic use of poets led Kṣemā to encounter the Buddha, initiating her path to enlightenment."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-25T08:42:35Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45574441641", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Kṣemā's attainment of arhatship provided a powerful example for women seeking spiritual liberation within the early Buddhist Sangha.", "Her reno